[Unit]
Description=Stratton Oakmont paper book -- the indicator's own signal, on the coins that move
After=stratton-oakmont-chrome.service
# Wants, not Requires.
#
# Requires propagates a restart: every time the guard restarted the browser to
# reclaim its memory -- which it does deliberately, with the comment "restart
# it, not the book" -- systemd stopped and restarted this unit along with it,
# mid-position. Wants still pulls chrome in at boot and After still orders
# them; what it drops is the propagation.
Wants=stratton-oakmont-chrome.service

[Service]
User=stratton
WorkingDirectory=/home/stratton-oakmont/bot
# --source combo, not break.
#
# `break` runs breakout(), a detector this engine builds itself: a quiet band,
# then one candle out of it. Measured over 41 hours and 70 coins it reached
# its target 2.5% of the time and stopped 85% of the time, and no target size
# rescued it. The reason is structural -- it needs a band that has gone quiet,
# and a coin in a powerful move never goes quiet, so it was only ever finding
# shapes on coins that were not going anywhere. Sitting on the eleven biggest
# movers in the market for fifty hours produced eleven of its signals, zero of
# which reached the target.
#
# `combo` reads the signal the indicator itself publishes -- the same one on
# the operator's own screenshots. Measured the same way, on 163 of its signals
# across the highest-ATR coins, entry at the next bar's open and any candle
# spanning both target and stop counted as a stop:
#
#     all 163 signals          25.8% reached +10%     +50.5% per trade
#     ATR >= 2.5%              30.5%                  +79.7%
#     agents >= 4              35.4%                 +106.3%
#     both                     37.0%                 +116.2%
#
# --min-agents 3 with --min-atr 2.5, which belong together and are not two
# separate dials. Four agents alone measured +106.3% a trade against +12.7% at
# three, so three looked like the junk tier -- but the difference is not the
# agent count, it is which coin the signal fired on. Splitting the 98
# three-agent signals by the coin's own ATR:
#
#     three agents, ATR under 2.5%    24 signals, NOTHING won      -106.0%
#     three agents, ATR 2.5% or more  74 signals, 19 won (25.7%)    +52.3%
#     three agents, ATR 4% or more    49 signals, 14 won (28.6%)    +72.7%
#
# So the floor is what makes three agents tradeable, and taking three without
# it is taking those 24. The indicator's own quality labels are no help here
# and would actively mislead: inside the three-agent set PEERLESS measured
# -31.0% and GOOD +87.5%, so tier is not gated on.
#
# The watchlist already has an ATR floor, but a watchlist is a list of coins
# to look at and this is a question about a signal. A coin can fall quiet
# between scans, and the two windows the book follows are not chosen by the
# finder at all.
#
# No --min-confidence. That scale was built for the break source's shape and
# scores nothing the combo signal carries; the indicator has already done the
# judging, and its own tier is not used as a gate either -- PEERLESS measured
# +55.5% and GOOD +75.4%, so the tier does not rank and filtering on it would
# only make the book smaller.
#
# --lev 40 and --sl 2.0 go together and neither can move alone. The stop
# ceiling is (1/lev - maintenance), so fifty times allows 1.5% and forty
# allows 2.0%. Two percent measured 25.8% against 17.2% at one percent, and
# the difference is not subtle: a 1.5% stop at 50x sits exactly on the
# liquidation line, where the exchange closes the position rather than the
# stop. A ten percent target at forty times is +395% of the margin committed.
#
# --per-coin-lev, and so --min-lev 0: every coin is traded at the most the
# exchange allows on it rather than at one number for all of them. The cap is
# lowest on exactly the coins the ATR scan likes best -- MAGMA, PONS and
# MARSCOIN at 20x, USELESS at 25 -- and a floor of forty threw all of them
# away. Now a 25x coin is traded at 25 and a 75x coin at 75.
#
# The leverage is still held to whatever leaves the stop inside the
# liquidation line. The exchange closes a position at about (1/leverage minus
# maintenance) of adverse price, so a 1.25% stop at 75x -- where that line
# sits at 0.83% -- would never be reached: the loss would be the whole margin
# at a worse price and the exit would be the exchange's choice, not ours. With
# a 1.25% stop and 0.5% maintenance the ceiling is about 57x, and a coin that
# allows more is traded at 57.
#
# --lev 40 is what a coin whose cap cannot be read is traded at, and the
# starting point when --per-coin-lev is off.
#
# --tp 5, not 10. This is the operator's strategy, and one trade made the case
# for it better than any measurement could.
#
# SELL 4USDT, opened 10:45 on 2026-09-05 against a ten percent target:
#
#     11:30   +5.34%   45 minutes in -- a five percent target pays here
#     11:45   +7.35%
#     12:00   +8.40%   the peak
#     12:10   +6.11%
#     12:30   +4.04%
#     13:20   +2.66%   two and a half hours in, still open, still waiting
#
# It was never going to reach ten. It reached five in forty-five minutes,
# spent thirty more between six and eight, and then gave all of it back over
# the next hour while the book held on for a target the move did not have in
# it. The strategy is to be paid by a pump and leave, not to sit through the
# round trip.
#
# The measured cost is real and worth stating: over 163 of the indicator's own
# signals a five percent target reached its mark 31.3% of the time against
# 25.8% at ten, but each win is half the size, so the measured expectancy per
# trade fell. That measurement assumes a trade held to target or stop with
# nothing in between -- which is exactly what the counter-trend exit and this
# shorter target are for. What it cannot price is the trade above: eight
# percent in front and closed by hand at less than one.
#
# --sl 1.25, cut with the target rather than left behind it. Halving one
# without the other halves only the wins: at a 5% target with the old 2% stop
# every win was cut in half while every loss stayed the same size, and the
# break-even win rate went from 16.7% to 28.6% against a signal that achieves
# about 31%. Over 163 of the indicator's own signals at a 5% target:
#
#     stop 0.80%   R:R 6.2   17.2% reached target   +3.3% a trade
#     stop 1.00%   R:R 5.0   20.9%                  +5.9%
#     stop 1.25%   R:R 4.0   24.5%                  +7.3%   <- here
#     stop 1.50%   R:R 3.3   26.4%                  +4.6%
#     stop 2.00%   R:R 2.5   31.3%                  +3.9%
#
# Tighter than this and the stop starts catching noise rather than a wrong
# signal; wider and the reward stops paying for the losses. Four to one is the
# floor a test enforces, and this sits exactly on it.
#
# --with-trend refuses a signal pointing against the coin's own last two
# hours. Same 163 signals: with the coin, 20.7% reached target for +5.3% a
# trade; against it, 13.0% and -12.1%. And a coin that had just fallen more
# than five percent produced thirteen signals and won NONE of them -- which is
# exactly what BUY TRIAUSDT was, a long taken two candles after a -6.67% drop,
# stopped twelve minutes later for -$81.
#
# One position at a time, half the wallet, and the book chooses.
#
# --max-exposure 0.5 with --frac 0.5 is what makes it one: a second position
# would need another half and there is not one. So the book is never in two
# places, and every trade is the whole size.
#
# --day-start 17.5: the day runs from nine in the evening in Tehran, not from
# midnight UTC. The budget is a day's budget and the day is the operator's --
# a boundary in the middle of their evening would reset the count halfway
# through the session they are actually watching.
#
# --per-day 8 is a budget, not a cap, and the difference is the whole design.
#
# A cap refuses the ninth signal whatever it is, and the ninth is as likely to
# be the best one of the day as the first. A fixed floor is no better: on a
# rich day it leaves the budget unspent, on a poor day it is gone by noon.
#
# So the book keeps every score it has seen for a day and works out two things
# from it -- how often signals arrive, and how good they usually are. Then the
# bar it demands is whatever percentile it can afford:
#
#     slots left / signals still expected today  =  the fraction affordable
#
# Eight slots against eighty expected signals means it can afford the top
# tenth, so the bar is the ninetieth percentile of what the day has offered.
# Two slots with an hour left and three signals coming means it can afford two
# in three, and the bar falls to the thirty-third. A generous day makes it
# fussy; a thin one makes it patient; and the budget lands on eight of the
# day's best rather than the day's first eight or none at all.
#
# --min-entry 40 is the floor beneath all of it. "The best available" is not a
# reason to take something bad because the day was poor, and it is also what
# the book uses before it has enough history to pace on -- its first morning
# has to be able to trade at all.
#
# --max-per-day 0 keeps the old blunt cap switched off. The budget replaced
# it.
#
# --min-entry 55 is that judgement, out of a hundred, and every term in it was
# measured at the signal bar over 163 of the indicator's own signals:
#
#     agents          40 pts   four or more reached target 35.4% of the time
#                              against 19.4% at three
#     a calm coin     25 pts   the calmer half won 23.9% for +12.7% a trade,
#                              the wilder half 10.1% for -20.5% -- a 1.25%
#                              stop on a wild coin is taken out by noise
#     six hours       15 pts   the longer move with the trade: +7.4% / -14.4%
#     one hour        10 pts   the shorter move NOT already spent our way
#     volume           5 pts   the candle carrying more than its own normal
#     travel           5 pts   ATR enough to reach five percent at all
#
# They add to exactly a hundred, so the floor is a fraction of something
# reachable rather than of a number nobody can hit.
#
# Fifty-five cannot be reached on agents alone, nor on a pile of small terms:
# it needs the conviction AND the coin. A reading the finder does not have
# scores nothing either way, because missing is not bad.
#
# Built for eight winning trades a day, which is what the target now is.
#
# The supply is there and was never the problem: 163 of the indicator's own
# signals over fifty hours on eleven coins is 78 a day, and the watchlist
# holds ten of them. At the 24.5% these settings reach, eight winners needs
# thirty-three trades taken -- about 42% of what prints.
#
# Three things were stopping that, and all three were ours, not the market's:
#
#   --max-per-day 4    four trades cannot contain eight winners
#   --frac 0.5         two slots, so a third signal was refused for exposure
#   the gates          which stay, because they are what makes 24.5%
#
# So the cap goes to 40, which is above anything a day should reach and is
# there only to stop a runaway, and the size goes to a fifth of the wallet:
# five positions at once instead of two, each stop costing a tenth of the
# account rather than a quarter. Smaller size is not caution here, it is what
# lets the book be in five places at the same time -- eight winners cannot be
# collected two at a time.
#
# The perch is the other half of the same idea. atrscan keeps the biggest
# candles in the market, the scout walks only those, perch.py puts the book's
# own window over whichever of them is closest to printing, and the book takes
# that print the moment it lands. Sitting over the coin rather than passing
# over it is what turns four trades a day into four good ones.
#
# --ct-exit 3.5 moves with the target. At a ten percent target 6.5 was two
# thirds of the way; at five, the same fraction is about 3.5. An exit above
# the target can never fire, and one too near it fires on noise.
#
# --ct-exit is the one exit that is neither the target nor the stop.
#
# The target is ten percent and a trade six or seven percent up is most of the
# way there. A counter-trend print is the indicator saying, on its own chart,
# that the move being ridden is being taken back -- and riding that back down
# to a two percent stop turns a trade that was worth +260% of its margin into
# one worth -80%. It never fires on a losing trade and never on a print in our
# own direction: it exists to keep a profit, not to cut a loss early, and the
# stop is where it is for a reason.
#
# The scout is what sees it. The book watches one window; the scout walks the
# whole watchlist and writes down every counter-trend print it passes, which
# is the only way a position on a coin nobody is looking at can be warned.
#
# The council is not consulted. It reads "no plan" on the very charts these
# signals fire on.
#
# The 50% rule (2026-09-07, the operator's 12-hour test):
#
#   --tp-margin 0.50     the target pays 50% of the margin on EVERY trade,
#                        fixed. The price distance is 50/lev % -- decided by
#                        this trade's own leverage -- so the payment never
#                        moves whatever the coin.
#   --sl-atr 1.0         the stop is the coin's own ATR: every trade stops
#                        at its own coin's scale instead of one number for
#                        all of them.
#   --per-coin-lev       the leverage is the exchange's cap for that coin,
#                        held inside the liquidation line for THAT stop --
#                        the three are computed together so the 50% payment
#                        stays exact.
#
#   --tp 5 --sl 1.25 stay as the fallback for a coin whose ATR cannot be
#   read, and everything else is the setup we fixed together. The
#   asymmetry is stated, not hidden: a stop costs roughly half the margin
#   at the stop's own scale while a win pays exactly half the margin, so
#   the book needs a high hit rate -- which is precisely what this easy
#   target is meant to buy, and what the 12 hours will measure.
ExecStart=/home/stratton-oakmont/venv/bin/python -u /home/stratton-oakmont/bot/papertrade.py \
  --source combo --scout --skip-window 1 --entry edge \
  --interval 2.0 --sprint 0.2 --stack \
  --tp 5 --lev 40 --frac 0.5 --sl 1.25 --max-exposure 0.5 \
  --sl-atr 1.0 --tp-margin 0.50 \
  --tp-atr 0 --tp-r 0 --tp-leg 0 --r-adapt 0 --risk-pct 0 \
  --min-confidence 0 --min-agents 3 --min-atr 2.5 --with-trend \
  --per-coin-lev \
  --min-entry 40 --min-lev 0 \
  --per-day 0 --day-start 17.5 --max-per-day 0 --fill-bars 8 \
  --step-at 10000 --step-frac 0.25 --break-even 0 --ct-exit 3.5 \
  --filter-model /home/stratton-oakmont/bot/data/dataset/samples/model.npz --filter-min 0.485
Restart=always
RestartSec=8
# Whatever the kernel kills when the box runs out of memory, it must not be
# this. The browser holding the charts grows about 80 MB an hour and is by far
# the largest thing here (3.2 GB of a 3.9 GB box after 31 hours), so memory
# pressure is a question of when, not if. Every chrome renderer already scores
# higher than the book does, but "already" is not a guarantee -- and the two
# processes are not equivalent losses. A killed renderer costs a chart, which
# the guard reloads; a killed book, mid-position, costs the position's
# management until systemd brings it back. This makes the ordering explicit
# rather than incidental.
OOMScoreAdjust=-500

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
